#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199492 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something.\n\nThe **mass** of an object measures how much \"stuff\" (or **matter**) an object contains. The mass of an object is connected to its weight. We use **mass units** to specify an object's mass.\n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit of mass is the **gram,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{g}$.\" The object below has a mass of exactly $1\\,\\textrm g$.\n\n![](images/dd/dd6ce9605969965944c1b9a7a87e4064bc1c64fb7195ee58b27e744a5e6dfd2b.png)\n\nWe can measure the mass of an object using **weighing scales.** To measure an object's mass, we first place it on one side of the weighing scales. Then, we put $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the other side until the scales balance perfectly.\n\n![](images/dd/dd4da91ec2ee27333843532cbf303da3dbe5eac388c8b14b538b8852159b0f76.png)\n\nIn this example, we have an object on the left scale and sixteen $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the right scale. Since the scales are perfectly balanced, the object's mass is $16\\,\\textrm g$.\n\nIt's convenient to define new units of measurement from the base unit. The most common variations for mass units are shown below:\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest (or \"heaviest\") unit $(\\textrm{kg})$ to the smallest (or \"lightest\") unit $(\\textrm{mg})$:\n\n- A regular bag of sugar has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{kg}$.\n- A single postage stamp has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm g$.\n- A single grain of sand has a mass of around $50\\,\\textrm{mg}$.\n\n**Important**: Each metric symbol for mass ends in \"$\\textrm{g}$\", the base unit." :tutorial/id #uuid "6d1214d5-e0a9-5ffd-8b79-73c1b78305b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7782 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199496 :tutorial/content "Another way to measure mass is to use **customary units**. The most common customary units of mass are **ounce**, **pound**, and **ton**.\n\nA table showing some of the most common customary mass units is shown below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Ton | $\\textrm{ton}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{ton} = 2,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$ |\n| Pound | $\\textrm{lb}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{lb} = 16\\,\\textrm{oz}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\textrm{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{ton})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{oz})$:\n\n- A small car could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{ton}$.\n- A small bag of rice has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{lb}$.\n- A single slice of bread could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{oz}$.\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries." :tutorial/id #uuid "71c4ce6d-783d-5c7a-ac8e-015cc5bde4c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7798 :tutorial/title "Customary Units of Mass"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199514 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above.\n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$.\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mg}=\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm{g}$. Notice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{g}{:}$\n$1mg + 1mg + ⋯ + 1mg^(⏞)^(1000times)|=; \\frac{1}{1000}g + \\frac{1}{1000}g + ⋯ + \\frac{1}{1000}g|=; 1000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1000}g|=; \\frac{1000 \\cdot 1}{1000}g|=; \\frac{1000}{1000}g|=; 1g$\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{g}$, as stated in the table." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c656aea-de27-5e2c-9a0f-d9a229d4e0c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7944 :tutorial/title "The Prefix System for Metric Units"}]}
